<body>

<h1>AI Internal Linking</h1>
<p class="sub">What the plugin does, how it decides, and what it will never do. Version 0.15.1.</p>

<h2>In one paragraph</h2>

<p>The plugin reads your whole site, works out which pages should link to which, and proposes each link with the exact wording to use and the sentence it would sit in. You approve or reject each one. Approved links are written into the content with two independent ways to undo them. It runs on any WordPress site, needs no configuration and no API key, and has an optional AI layer on top if you want one.</p>

<h2>How a link gets suggested</h2>

<p>Three engines run in order on every page. Each draws from the same link budget, so the strongest evidence wins.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Order</th><th>Engine</th><th>The evidence it uses</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Search Console: a query the target page already ranks for, said on this page without a link</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>A chat model reading the page and a shortlist of genuinely related pages</td><td>Your own API key</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Related Content</td><td>Vocabulary overlap, computed locally from your own content</td><td>Free</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Search demand is stronger evidence than similarity, which is why the keyword engine runs first. The free engine runs last and fills whatever budget is left, so you always get suggestions even with no key configured.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>The rule behind all three:</strong> the plugin links words that are already on your page. It never writes new sentences, never invents an anchor, and never inserts anything you have not approved.</div>

<h2>How the plugin reads your content</h2>

<p>A fair question, and the answer decides how fast it is, what it can see, and whether anything leaves your server.</p>

<p><strong>It reads your content from inside WordPress, from the database. It never visits your website.</strong></p>

<p>There is a difference between the two, and it matters:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it means</th><th>Used here?</th></tr>
<tr><td>Internal (this plugin)</td><td>Reads the post content straight out of the database, the same text you see in the editor, then converts it to plain words</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>External (how a crawler or an AI bot works)</td><td>Requests your public page over the internet and reads the finished HTML a visitor would receive</td><td>No, never</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The plugin asks WordPress for each post directly. For page builders it reads the builder's own stored data (Elementor, Beaver Builder and ACF keep their content in post fields). It then strips out the HTML tags, the scripts and styles, and every heading, leaving plain words to work with.</p>

<h3>What that gets you</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fast</td><td>No page requests. Reading 350 pages from the database takes seconds, not minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zero load on your site</td><td>Visitors are never competing with a crawler for your server</td></tr>
<tr><td>Works on private sites</td><td>Staging sites, password protected sites and sites behind a firewall all index normally</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caching cannot mislead it</td><td>It always sees the current content, never a stale cached copy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drafts and private posts are readable</td><td>An external crawler could never see them</td></tr>
<tr><td>The words it finds are words you can edit</td><td>Every anchor it proposes exists in your editor, so applying a link is a precise change to your own text</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The trade-off, stated honestly</h3>

<p>Because it reads the stored content rather than the finished page, it does not see things that only appear when a page is displayed: text your theme adds around the article, related-post blocks generated at display time, or the output of a shortcode belonging to another plugin. Shortcode tags themselves are removed and any text written between them is kept, but whatever a shortcode would have generated is not indexed.</p>

<p>In practice this is the right trade. The plugin should only ever link words that live in your content, because those are the words you can edit, and a link placed in theme-generated furniture would vanish the moment you changed themes.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>What this means for privacy.</strong> With no API key configured, your content never leaves your server at all: there is nothing to send and nowhere to send it. If you switch on the optional AI engine, the part of each page you allow is sent to the one provider you chose, using your own key, and to nobody else. Google Search Console is the only other outside connection, and that pulls your own search data in rather than sending content out.</div>

<h2>The formulas, engine by engine</h2>

<p>Each engine answers a different question, and each produces a <strong>relevance</strong> score between 0 and 1. Everything after that is shared. They appear here in the order that matters most, ending with the engine that needs no key, no internet connection and no budget.</p>

<h3>Engine 1 of 3: AI Suggestion</h3>

<p class="sub">The question it answers: "reading this page, which of these related pages would a careful editor link to, and using which words?"</p>

<p>The model never browses your site and never chooses freely. It is handed a closed shortlist and asked to pick from it.</p>

<pre class="formula">shortlist   = the 18 best matches from the free engine, trimmed to 15
page sent   = the first N words          (your setting, default 1,000)
reply limit = short, about 600 words
creativity  = turned off, so the same page always gives the same answer

relevance   = the model's own confidence, 0 to 1
              (0.7 is assumed if the model does not supply one)</pre>

<p><strong>In plain words:</strong> the free engine draws up the shortlist; the model only decides which of them deserve a link and what wording to use. Its confidence in that choice becomes the score.</p>

<p><strong>How it actually picks the page and the wording.</strong> More constrained than "the AI chooses a link" suggests. The model is shown a numbered list of page titles, and it replies with a number:</p>

<pre class="formula">SHOWN TO THE MODEL                    THE MODEL REPLIES

ARTICLE TITLE: Build a Content Calendar   {"n": 1,
ARTICLE TEXT: ...your content marketing    "anchor": "content marketing strategy",
strategy stays consistent...               "reason": "the article plans a strategy",
                                           "confidence": 0.86}
POSSIBLE DESTINATIONS:
  1. Content Marketing Strategy: A Guide
  2. Social Media Scheduling Tools
  3. Editorial Workflow for Small Teams</pre>

<p>Notice what is missing: <strong>no web addresses anywhere</strong>. The model picks number 1. The plugin then looks up which page that is and takes its address from its own index. The model never handles a URL, so it cannot invent one.</p>

<p>Then the quoted phrase is searched for in your article. If it is not there, the whole suggestion is thrown away. If it is there, <strong>the version saved is the one from your article, not the one the model typed</strong>. The model wrote "content marketing strategy" in lower case; your page says "Content Marketing Strategy", so that is what is stored and that is what gets linked.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Decision</th><th>Made by</th></tr>
<tr><td>Which pages are even eligible</td><td>The free engine, before the model is asked</td></tr>
<tr><td>Which of them to link, and roughly what wording</td><td>The model</td></tr>
<tr><td>The exact anchor text saved</td><td>Your article, by exact match</td></tr>
<tr><td>The web address</td><td>The plugin's index</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Three checks make a fabricated link impossible:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Check</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>The page it chose must be one of the 15 it was shown</td><td>Discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td>The wording it chose must already appear in your page, word for word</td><td>Discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headings and the title are stripped before the model sees the text</td><td>Cannot be chosen at all</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>The one limit worth knowing.</strong> The model reads the first N words, not the whole page. At the default of 1,000 words, a 3,000 word article is roughly one third visible, so its suggestions come from the opening section. Raise "Words per page sent to the AI" on Setup and Dashboard to widen it. This limit applies to this engine only.</div>

<h3>The whole decision, as a flowchart</h3>

<p>Three things get settled, in this order: which page is being read, which page it should link to, and which words become the link. Every branch that throws a suggestion away is shown, because those are usually the interesting ones.</p>

<p><img src="ai-selection-flowchart.png" alt="How the AI picks the page and the wording" style="width:100%; max-width:1180px; height:auto;" /></p>
<p class="sub">Green boxes are decisions that have been settled. Amber boxes on the right are the points where a suggestion is thrown away.</p>

<h3>Why not the other page, and why not other wording</h3>

<p>Every rejection above has a reason. Read together they explain almost every "why did it not suggest X?" question:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>What was rejected</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A different source page</strong></td><td>Nothing was rejected. Every published page in your chosen content types is read in turn. A page is only skipped if it has already reached its link limit, which protects pages that are well linked already</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pages outside the closest 18</strong></td><td>They share too little vocabulary with this page. Sending all 350 to the model would cost roughly twenty times more and produce worse answers, because a long list invites weak picks</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A page you already link to</strong></td><td>The link exists. Suggesting it again is noise</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A pair you already judged</strong></td><td>You approved or rejected it before. It is not offered a second time</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The page itself</strong></td><td>A page cannot link to itself</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A page number that was not on the list</strong></td><td>The model can only choose from what it was shown. Anything else is meaningless and is discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wording that is not on the page</strong></td><td>The single most important rejection. If the quoted phrase cannot be found in your article, the whole suggestion is discarded. This is why the plugin can use an AI model without ever letting it write into your content</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wording inside a heading</strong></td><td>It never gets that far. Headings and the page title are stripped out before the model is shown anything, so it cannot quote them</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wording the model tidied up</strong></td><td>If the model rephrases you, its version will not be found in your page, so it is discarded. Your text is the only text that can become a link</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="key"><strong>The short answer to "why this and not that".</strong> The plugin narrows the choice before the model is asked, and checks the answer against your actual page afterwards. The model only ever chooses within limits already set, and every one of its answers is verified against something the plugin already knows.</div>

<h3>Engine 2 of 3: GSC keyword</h3>

<p class="sub">The question it answers: "this page says a phrase another page already ranks for on Google, and does not link to it. Why not?"</p>

<p>This is the strongest evidence available, because it comes from real search demand rather than from similarity. It runs first.</p>

<p><strong>Step one, what is this keyword worth?</strong></p>

<pre class="formula">gain        = click rate at position 3, minus click rate where you rank now
              (never below zero)
reach       = 1          if you rank 20 or better
            = 20 / rank  if you rank worse than 20

opportunity = impressions * gain * reach</pre>

<p><strong>In plain words:</strong> the extra clicks this keyword could realistically win, discounted by how believable that jump is. Already in the top 3? Nothing to gain, so the score is zero. Ranked 50th? One internal link will not fix that, so the score is cut down.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>You rank at</th><th>1</th><th>3</th><th>5</th><th>10</th><th>20</th><th>40</th><th>99</th></tr>
<tr><td>Score per 1,000 impressions</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>50</td><td>85</td><td>102</td><td>53</td><td>21</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The sweet spot is positions 5 to 20, known as <strong>striking distance</strong>: high enough that Google already takes the page seriously, low enough that there is real room to climb.</p>

<p><strong>Step two, turn that into a relevance score:</strong></p>

<pre class="formula">relevance = 0.50                                    always
          + 0.20   if you rank between 5 and 20
          + 0.28 * (this keyword / your best keyword)
          capped at 0.98</pre>

<p>The floor of 0.50 is deliberate. A proven search phrase is better evidence than any similarity measure, so these suggestions start above what the other engines normally reach.</p>

<p><strong>How it actually picks the page and the wording.</strong> Neither half is chosen by the plugin. Search Console hands over one row per query:</p>

<pre class="formula">query        content marketing strategy
page         /guides/content-marketing-strategy/
impressions  1,200
position     7</pre>

<p>The address is matched to one of your posts, and <strong>that post is the destination</strong>, chosen by Google's index rather than by any scoring here. <strong>The wording is the query itself</strong>, chosen by the people who typed it. The plugin then scans your pages for that exact phrase and flags the ones that say it without linking. If a page already links there, or the same wording already points there three times, it is skipped.</p>

<p>As with the AI engine, the anchor saved is the text as it appears in your page, so your capitalisation wins over Google's lower case.</p>

<h3>Who decides what, across all three</h3>

<p>The three engines differ in one important way: who chooses the destination, and who chooses the wording.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Engine</th><th>Chooses the destination</th><th>Chooses the wording</th><th>In what order</th></tr>
<tr><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>The model, by picking a number from a shortlist the free engine built</td><td>The model quotes a phrase; your page supplies the exact text</td><td>Both together</td></tr>
<tr><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Google, through Search Console</td><td>Searchers, it is the phrase they typed</td><td>The destination arrives attached to the keyword</td></tr>
<tr><td>Related Content</td><td>Vocabulary overlap, calculated on your own site</td><td>The destination page's own title</td><td>Destination first, wording worked out afterwards</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Whatever the source, one rule closes every path: <strong>the wording must already exist in your page</strong>, matched as a whole word and ignoring capitals. If it is not there, no suggestion is made. That single check is what guarantees the plugin never writes a new sentence, and it is applied after the engine has had its say, not before.</p>

<p>It also means the anchor stored is always <em>your</em> text. Whatever the model typed, or however Google reported the query, what gets saved and linked is the phrase exactly as it appears in your article.</p>

<h3>The two scores on every suggestion</h3>

<pre class="formula">naturalness = 0.45
            + 0.30 * relevance
            + 0.15   if the wording is more than one word
            + 0.10   if it is 8 to 60 characters long
            - 0.20   if it is shorter than 5 characters
            - 0.15   if it is longer than 70 characters

confidence  = 0.60 * relevance + 0.40 * naturalness</pre>

<p><strong>In plain words:</strong> relevance asks "should these two pages be connected at all", naturalness asks "does this exact wording read well here". The number shown in the review inbox blends the two, with relevance carrying most of the weight.</p>

<h3>Engine 3 of 3: Related Content</h3>

<p class="sub">The question it answers: "which pages talk about the same things as this one?" No key, no internet, no cost.</p>

<p>This engine is always available and fills whatever link budget the other two leave. It also builds the shortlist the AI reads, so it is working even when the AI is switched on.</p>

<p><strong>Step one, decide which words matter.</strong> Each page is reduced to its 300 most used words, filler words removed. Every word is then weighted by how rare it is across your whole site:</p>

<pre class="formula">weight of a word = ln(1 + total pages / pages containing it)

A word appearing on more than 40 percent of your
pages is dropped entirely.</pre>

<p><strong>In plain words:</strong> a word that appears everywhere on your site tells you nothing about which two pages belong together. A rare word tells you a great deal. So rare words count for more, and site-wide words are ignored outright.</p>

<p><strong>Step two, compare two pages:</strong></p>

<pre class="formula">source words = the 40 most used words on this page

overlap   = for each shared word, its weight times the smaller
            of the two pages' counts, added up

relevance = overlap / this page's own total weight
            (at least 2 shared meaningful words required)</pre>

<p><strong>In plain words:</strong> the score is the share of this page's vocabulary that the other page also covers. Taking the <em>smaller</em> of the two counts is what stops a page that repeats one word two hundred times from looking relevant to everything.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>Nothing here knows your subject.</strong> The vocabulary is whatever your own pages contain, and the weights are calculated from your own site at scan time. The identical formula produces recipe links on a recipe site and legal links on a law firm site, with no configuration.</div>

<p><strong>How it actually picks the page and the wording.</strong> This engine works in the opposite order to the other two: it settles the destination first, then tries to work out the wording, and gives up on the pair if it cannot.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Every other page is scored on vocabulary overlap and sorted, best first</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>The best remaining page is taken, skipping the page itself and anything already linked, suggested or rejected</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>That page's <strong>title</strong> is cut at punctuation and broken into word groups, 4 words down to 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Groups starting or ending on a filler word, or containing bare numbers, are thrown away</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>The longest surviving group that appears in your page becomes the anchor</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>If none of them appears, <strong>the whole pairing is abandoned</strong> and the next page is tried</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>This is the most common reason an expected link is missing.</strong> Two pages can be plainly about the same subject and still produce nothing, simply because your text never happens to contain a phrase from the other page's title. The other two engines are not bound by that, which is much of why they run first.</div>

<h3>The limits every suggestion must pass</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Limit</th><th>Default</th><th>Why it exists</th></tr>
<tr><td>Links per 1,000 words</td><td>5</td><td>Stops a page becoming a link farm</td></tr>
<tr><td>New suggestions per page</td><td>8</td><td>Keeps a review session manageable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wording length</td><td>2 to 4 words</td><td>Descriptive, never a whole sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Same wording to the same page</td><td>3</td><td>Avoids looking manipulative to a search engine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum relevance</td><td>0.08</td><td>Discards weak vocabulary matches</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>A worked example, start to finish</h3>

<p>Your page mentions the phrase "content marketing strategy". Another page is titled "Content Marketing Strategy: A Complete Guide".</p>

<pre class="formula">1. The title splits at the colon, giving "Content Marketing Strategy".
2. That 3 word phrase is found in your page, so it can be the wording.
3. Say the vocabulary overlap scored     relevance   = 0.42

4. naturalness = 0.45 + (0.30 x 0.42)              = 0.576
               + 0.15  more than one word          = 0.726
               + 0.10  26 characters, within range = 0.826

5. confidence  = (0.60 x 0.42) + (0.40 x 0.826)    = 0.58</pre>

<p>It reaches the review inbox showing 0.42 relevance, 0.83 naturalness and 0.58 overall, with the surrounding sentence quoted so you can judge the placement before approving anything.</p>

<h2>What it will never do</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Never</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>Insert a link without approval</td><td>Every suggestion waits in a review inbox. Nothing reaches your content until you click.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invent anchor text</td><td>The anchor must already appear, word for word, in the page. If it does not, no suggestion is made at all.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link inside a heading</td><td>Headings and the page title are stripped before the plugin reads anything, so they cannot be linked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link across languages</td><td>An English page is never linked to a Hindi one, on any multilingual setup.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rewrite builder content</td><td>Elementor, Divi, WPBakery, Beaver Builder and ACF pages are suggest-only. The plugin never touches content a builder owns.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Over-link a page</td><td>A density ceiling per 1,000 words, a per-page cap, and a limit on repeating the same exact anchor to one target.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Send your content anywhere without a key</td><td>With no API key configured, the plugin makes no external calls at all.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Applying a link, and undoing it</h2>

<p>Approving a link does write to your post. That is the point of the feature, so the safety around it is worth stating precisely.</p>

<p>Before a single character changes, the plugin saves a WordPress revision AND writes the original content to its own backup ledger. Two independent ways back, because a revision can be pruned by another plugin and the ledger cannot.</p>

<p>The insertion itself is a byte-preserving splice: the plugin finds the exact phrase and wraps it, rather than parsing your HTML and writing it back out. That distinction matters, because rebuilding HTML from a parser quietly corrupts real world markup. Every write is checked before saving, to confirm the visible text is unchanged.</p>

<p>Inserted links are plain <code>&lt;a data-ailinking-id&gt;</code> tags, never shortcodes. If the plugin were deleted from the filesystem tomorrow, what remains in your content is an ordinary working hyperlink, not broken markup. Uninstalling properly restores the original content from the ledger first.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>Undo comes in two sizes.</strong> One click reverses a single link. A batched action reverses every link the plugin has ever inserted, in one go. If a post was edited after a link was applied, undo warns you before overwriting those edits.</div>

<h2>What you see in the admin</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Tab</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Setup &amp; Dashboard</td><td>Which content to include, indexing, running a scan with pause, resume and stop, and how much of each page the AI reads</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suggestions</td><td>The review inbox: approve, reject, apply, undo, filtered by status and by which engine proposed it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link Health</td><td>Orphans, dead ends, broken links, over and under-linked pages, internal PageRank, anchor diversity, click depth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Connect GSC</td><td>Google Search Console, connected with a service account. Generic keyword CSV import as a fallback</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Keys</td><td>Your API keys, encrypted at rest, with token usage and estimated cost per key and per model</td></tr>
<tr><td>Settings</td><td>Engine toggles, link density, provider choice, monthly spend cap</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>If you use an API key</h2>

<p>Keys are yours. There is no vendor account, no proxy and no middleman: the plugin talks directly to whichever provider you configured, using your key. Keys are encrypted before storage, are never shown in full, are never written to logs, and are never sent to the browser.</p>

<h3>Knowing what it costs, before it costs it</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td>Live ticker</td><td>Tokens and estimated cost accumulating while a scan runs, so you watch the spend rather than discover it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usage breakdown</td><td>This month and all time, per key and per model, on the AI Keys tab</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monthly spend cap</td><td>Stops AI calls before the cap is exceeded. The free engines keep working</td></tr>
<tr><td>Words per page</td><td>How much of each page the AI reads. The main cost lever, since every post in a scan is one request</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>The AI reads part of a page, not all of it.</strong> By default the first 1,000 words, so on a long article the AI engine proposes links from the opening section only. Raise "Words per page sent to the AI" on Setup and Dashboard to widen that: presets go to 3,000 words, and a custom value up to 20,000. This applies to the AI engine alone. The two free engines always read the entire page.</div>

<h2>Requirements</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>WordPress</th><td>6.2 or newer</td></tr>
<tr><th>PHP</th><td>7.4 or newer</td></tr>
<tr><th>API key</th><td>Optional. Everything except the AI engine works without one</td></tr>
<tr><th>Search Console</th><td>Optional. Improves suggestion quality when connected</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="footer">
